ROUTE NOTE for `integrable_growthFun_uminus` (including a trap worth knowing before starting).

* What it is.  `growthFun (Uminus t) ν t x = -log (ν x (conjBox (Uminus t) t)).toReal`, and
  `conjBox U t ⊆ Box1 U` with `IsLeafwise.normalised` (`ν x (Box1 U) = 1`) gives
  `0 ≤ growthFun` on `X'`.  So the obligation is *one-sided*: it says the leafwise measures do
  not put almost all their mass outside one fixed contracted box on a set of positive measure.

* The tempting first move is false.  `conjBox U t` is a box about the identity of positive
  radius, hence contains `V δ ∩ U` for some *uniform* `δ > 0` (use `V_hasBasis_nhds_one`), and
  `IsLeafwise.pos` gives `0 < ν x (V δ)` pointwise.  It does **not** give a uniform lower bound:
  a bound `ν x (conjBox U t) ≥ c > 0` for `μ`-a.e. `x` is **false in general**, because near the
  cusp the `U`-orbits are short (the injectivity radius degenerates), so the conditional
  measures can legitimately put `conjBox U t` at arbitrarily small mass on sets of positive
  measure.  Hence `growthFun` is *not* a.e. bounded and any proof of integrability must be
  cusp-aware — this is exactly why Pisa's §7.27–7.31 (thin boundaries, `μ(∂_δ P) ≤ c δ`) and the
  covering estimate of Lemma 7.46 exist, and why the `Uij` case is not a formal corollary of
  anything: the estimate is quantitative, not a compactness bound.

* Where the quantitative bound should come from.  The leafwise measures of interest are the
  patched family `Work.EKLRelCross.nuFull` (built from flower conditional measures with the
  `ProporPiece`/`PosPiece` inputs, Pisa 5.17 + 6.17 + 6.20).  The available shape of a usable
  bound is `-log ν x (conjBox U t) ≤ C + (an integrable function of x)` obtained by comparing
  `ν x (conjBox U t)` with the `μ`-mass of a flower atom of the same scale — the `propor`
  machinery (`EKLRelCross/plaqueDensity.lean`, `boxPlaque_density.lean`,
  `EKLRelCross/leafwise_propor.lean`) already contains the comparability of plaque mass with
  atom mass, and `EKLComp.box_mass_asymptotic*` contains the corresponding asymptotics (both
  currently take `hint` themselves, so care is needed to avoid circularity: the cell/`CellSys`
  estimates in `EKLC6Core/cellSys_thin_mass_decay.lean` are the `hint`-free ones).
  Since leafwise systems agree `μ`-a.e. (`P_Leafwise` (U1)), proving the bound for `nuFull`
  suffices for the universally quantified statement above.

* CORRECTION to that comparison (found while writing it).  `IsUBox U B` requires `B` to be
  *relatively open* in `U` (`B = O ∩ U` with `O` open), so `IsUBox U (Box1 U)` is **false** —
  `Box1` is closed and bounded in `U` — and likewise `IsUBox U (conjBox U t)`.  The boxes that do
  satisfy it are the neighbourhoods `V ε ∩ U` (`V ε = {g | ∀ i j, |g i j - 1 i j| < ε}`), and
  `Box1 U ⊆ V 2`.  So the usable comparison uses the *big* box `B := V 2 ∩ U` and the *small* box
  `B' := conjBox U t` (with `B' ⊆ Box1 U ⊆ B`, via `box_conjBox_subset`); `boxPlaque_density`
  then gives `μ (B' • x₀) / μ (B • x₀) = (ν x₀ B').toReal / (ν x₀ B).toReal`, and since
  `ν x₀ B ≥ ν x₀ (Box1 U) = 1` (`IsLeafwise.normalised` plus monotonicity) one gets the
  one-sided cusp estimate

    `μ (B' • x₀).toReal / μ (B • x₀).toReal ≤ (ν x₀ B').toReal`,

  i.e. `growthFun (Uminus t) ν t x₀ ≤ log μ (B • x₀).toReal - log μ (B' • x₀).toReal`.  That is
  the form the integrability obligation reduces to: an upper bound for
  `log μ (V 2 ∩ U) • x₀ − log μ (conjBox U t) • x₀` in `L¹(μ)`, which is a statement about the
  `μ`-masses of two nested orbit plaques and is where `cellSys_thin_mass_decay.lean` enters.
  The delegated `EKLC6Core/nu_conjBox_toReal_eq_mu_ratio` used the (unsatisfiable) `Box1` form
  and is superseded by `EKLC6Core/nu_conjBox_toReal_ge_mu_ratio`.  Confirming the diagnosis: that
  file was in fact *proved* — but only because its hypothesis `hBox₁ : IsUBox U (Box1 U)` is
  assumed, and `boxPlaque_density` needs exactly that (false) `IsUBox` datum to be applicable.
  So it is a theorem with unsatisfiable hypotheses: true, and unusable.  Do not consume it; the
  usable statement is the `V 2 ∩ U` one, which has no unsatisfiable hypothesis.

* SIDE CONDITIONS CLOSED (08:2x): `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/boxPlaque_measurable_conjBox.lean`
  proves both measurability hypotheses of the comparison, so they are no longer assumptions:
  `measurableSet_boxPlaque_conjBox` (the plaquette `conjBox U t • x₀` is the continuous image of
  the compact `conjBox U t` — no injectivity needed) and `measurableSet_boxPlaque_V`
  (`(V ε ∩ U) • x₀` is the standard orbit plaque, via `borelOrbitImage`).
  With those, `nu_conjBox_toReal_ge_mu_ratio` carries only the two non-degeneracy hypotheses
  `μ (plaque) ≠ 0`.

* **CAVEAT — and a second trap — on the plaque route (found while attacking the non-degeneracy).**
  The remaining hypothesis is NOT an a.e. fact and must not be delegated as one: for the Haar
  measure on `X₃` one has `μ (B • x₀) = 0` for every box `B`, because `U • x₀` is a
  `dim U`-dimensional submanifold of the `8`-dimensional `X₃` and is therefore Haar-null.  So the
  a.e. statement `μ ((V 2 ∩ U) • x₀) ≠ 0` is FALSE in general (and `hP₂` in the comparison is
  unused anyway).  Consequently `boxPlaque_density`, and with it this comparison, is a valid
  identity only in the non-degenerate regime where plaques carry positive `μ`-mass; it cannot be
  the general route to `Integrable (growthFun (Uminus t) ν t) μ`.
  The general route is the conditional-expectation one that the fleet's own chain already uses,
  and it never needs `μ (plaque) ≠ 0`: `atomInfo_ae_eq_aux_along` reads
  `growthFun (Uminus t) ν t (aDiag t • x) =
     atomInfo A U ν t x − log ν (aDiag t • x) (shape A U (aDiag t • x))
       + log ν x (shape A U x)`,
  i.e. `growthFun` is the martingale increment of `atomInfo` up to a coboundary.

  **WARNING, do not spend an agent on that identity as a route to integrability — it is an
  identity, not an estimate, and the route is circular.**  Because `growthFun (Uminus t) ν t
  (aDiag t • x) = atomInfo A U ν t x − (ψ (aDiag t • x) − ψ x)` with
  `ψ x := log (ν x (shape A U x)).toReal`, integrability of `growthFun` is *equivalent* to
  integrability of `atomInfo` together with `ψ` (the three terms differ by a coboundary, so
  neither inequality direction is available to bound one by the other).  This corrects an earlier
  note in this file which claimed the shape logarithms are "discharged by
  `HcondSig_eq_growth_free`": they are not.  `HcondSig_eq_growth_plaque` *assumes*
  `hψ : Integrable (fun x => Real.log ((ν x (shape A U x)).toReal)) μ`, and
  `HcondSig_eq_growth_free` avoids `hψ` only by assuming `hint : Integrable (growthFun …) μ`
  instead — it trades one integrability hypothesis for another and proves neither.  And `ψ` is
  the log of a leafwise mass of a bounded plaque, i.e. exactly the same kind of quantity as
  `growthFun`, so no progress is made.

  What remains is therefore a *direct* estimate on `ν x (conjBox (Uminus t) t)`, and the only
  route I can see is the cusp-aware one (thin boundaries, Pisa 7.27–7.31, plus the covering
  estimate of Lemma 7.46), with the caveat already recorded above that no uniform lower bound
  exists.  Pointers for whoever starts it: the contraction scale `V (Real.exp (-(α * n)) * ρ)`
  and its convergence bookkeeping already exist in
  `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/ae_ball_in_atom.lean` (§ around line 187) — that is Pisa 7.45/7.46
  vocabulary in place; the thin-boundary mass decay is
  `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/cellSys_thin_mass_decay.lean` (`cellSys_thin_mass_decay`,
  `cell_delta_lt_half_of_bad`); and `EKLC6Core/exists_isCompact_cover_inv_Uball.lean` has the
  compact-cover statement for `U`-balls.  Lemma 7.46 itself (a compact `Ω` is coverable by
  `c e^{κ n}` balls of radius `e^{-α n} r`) is not in the workspace in any form, and it is the
  first genuinely new lemma of that route — with PROGRESS as of this stretch: the *construction*
  half is proved in `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/exists_finset_cover_V.lean`
  (`V_plaque_mem_nhds`: `V δ • x ∈ 𝓝 x`, straight from `P_X3`; and `exists_finset_cover_V`:
  a compact `Ω` is covered by finitely many `V δ`-plaquettes, via interiors and compactness —
  counting-free).  The *counting* half is `Work.EKLC6Core.exists_cover_V_ball` (H80-L1298), delegated with
  the conventions pinned: `V δ`-plaques rather than metric balls, `κ = κ(α)` universal and
  `c = c(Ω, r)`, covering `Ω` by `#S ≤ c e^{κ n}` plaquettes of radius `e^{-α n} r`, and its
  flat ingredient is `Work.EKLC6Core.exists_cover_entryBox_ball` (H80-L1310):
  `[-M,M]^k` covered at scale `e^{-α n} r` by entrywise boxes with `#S ≤ c e^{κ n}`
  (witnesses `κ = α k`, `c = (2M/r + 1)^k`), which is counting in `ℝ^k` only.

  ATTACHMENT RECIPE for `exists_cover_V_ball`, so the last step is mechanical once both land:
  (1) fix a scale `δ₀` (e.g. `1/2`) and cover `Ω` by finitely many plaques `V δ₀ • y` with
  `exists_finset_cover_V` — this contributes only the `Ω`-dependent factor `c(Ω)`;
  (2) on each such plaque the orbit map is a chart (use the uniform-injectivity field of `P_X3`
  on the compact set), so covering the plaque at scale `e^{-α n} r` reduces to covering the
  entrywise parameter box, i.e. `exists_cover_entryBox_ball` with `k` = the number of parameters
  of `U` (at most `6` for the unipotent part, `9` for all matrix entries) — this is where the
  dimension enters, giving `κ = α·k`;
  (3) multiply the two factors and put the result in the shape `c e^{κ n}` — done as its own
  named lemma, DONE: `Submission/Work/EKLC6Core/exists_exp_bound_of_scale.lean` proves
  `exists_exp_bound_of_scale : (D e^{α n} + 1)^k ≤ c e^{κ n}` for all `n`, with the explicit
  witnesses `c = (D+1)^k`, `κ = α (k+1)`.  No case distinction on `n` is needed and none is made:
  `e^{α n} ≥ 1` for every `n ≥ 0` (not just for large `n`), so one constant absorbs the large
  scales and the exponential factor `e^{α k n}` absorbs the small ones.  This is the step that
  looks like it needs a "for large `n`" argument and does not, which is exactly why it was worth
  writing before the two counting halves landed.  The three pieces are independent: the
  construction half needs no estimate, the flat half needs no `X₃`, and this one needs neither;
  only step (2) references both.

  RECIPE for the flat half (`exists_cover_entryBox_ball`, in flight), so it is a mechanical
  assembly rather than a search:
  * one dimension: `[-M, M] ⊆ ⋃_{j < J+1} (y_j - ε, y_j + ε)` with `y_j := -M + ε + 2 j ε` and
    `J` the least natural with `M/ε ≤ J` (`Nat.find` on `exists_nat_ge`), so that
    `2 (J+1) ε ≥ 2 M` gives the cover and `J ≤ M/ε + 1` gives `#S ≤ 2M/ε + 2`;
  * `k` dimensions: the grid `S := (Finset.univ.pi (fun _ => Finset.range (J+1))).image (fun j i =>
    (j i : ℝ) * (2ε) - M + ε)` (`Finset.pi` for the product of index sets), so that
    `#S ≤ (J+1)^k ≤ (2M/ε + 2)^k` and the cover is coordinatewise;
  * finish with `exists_exp_bound_of_scale` applied with `D := 2M/r + 1` after rewriting
    `2M/(e^{-αn} r) + 2 ≤ 2 (2M/r + 1) e^{αn}`, which is the same `e^{αn} ≥ 1` step — note the
    factor `2` and the `+2` are absorbed by `D`, so no separate case on `n` appears anywhere.
  The remaining Lean frictions to expect: `Nat.find` gives the least `J` but its bound
  `J ≤ M/ε + 1` must be extracted via `Nat.find_min'` with the explicit witness
  `Nat.floor (M/ε) + 1`; and `Finset.pi`'s membership lemma is `Finset.mem_pi`.
  ONE SUBTLETY worth knowing before writing the one-dimensional grid, because a grid at the
  wrong spacing fails at exactly one point: with centres spaced `2ε` and open intervals of
  radius `ε` the point `y_j - ε` lies in *neither* `(y_j - ε, y_j + ε)` nor
  `(y_{j+1} - ε, y_{j+1} + ε)`, so such a grid does not cover.  Spacing `≤ 2ε` strictly fixes it:
  at spacing `ε` the point `y_j - ε` lies in `(y_{j-1} - ε, y_{j-1} + ε)` since
  `y_{j-1} - ε = y_j - 2ε < y_j - ε < y_j = y_{j-1} + ε`, and the endpoints `±M` are interior to
  the first and last intervals.  The one-dimensional cover is delegated as
  `Work.EKLC6Core.exists_finset_cover_Icc` (`|x| ≤ M` covered by `#S ≤ 2M/ε + 2` open
  `ε`-intervals), whose reviewer supplied the construction `S = {-M + k ε}` with `N` the least
  natural with `-M + N ε ≥ M` and the count `N + 1 ≤ 2M/ε + 1`.  The plaque comparison stays on file as a correct identity in the non-degenerate
  regime and as documentation of why the naive non-degeneracy cannot be used.
-/

